{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_42_765437451#6_1653240281","relevance":2,"reason":"This HealthTap result is directly about whether leukocytosis is dangerous and says it depends on the underlying cause, with infection/inflammation often resolving and malignant causes possible. However, it is not focused on WBC over 15,000 or untreated management.","learned":"Elevated WBC/leukocytosis is not inherently dangerous by itself; risk depends on the cause, ranging from reactive infection/inflammation to leukemia."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ leukocytosis \"15,000\" (+ severe serious) (+ infection treatment))","reason":"Search for concise medical evidence tying the 15,000 leukocytosis threshold to severity and treatment, which may directly support whether leaving it untreated can be dangerous."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ leukocytosis \"15,000\" (+ severe serious) (+ infection treatment))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_781802599#10_1611774069","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2924","rank":2,"snippet":"examination may demonstrate lower abdominal tenderness. Lower gastrointestinal endoscopy (flexible proctoscopy, sigmoidoscopy, or colonoscopy) examination may be normal or demonstrate a spectrum of findings, from patchy, mild erythema and friability to severe pseudomembranous colitis (severe inflammation of the inner lining of the bowel). ( See 'Endoscopy' below.) Unexplained leukocytosis in hospitalized patients (even in the absence of diarrhea) may reflect underlying CDI. In a prospective study of 60 patients with unexplained leukocytosis (white blood cell count ><cover>15,000 cells/mL), positive stool C. difficile toxin was observed in 58 percent of cases compared with 12 percent in controls [ 5 ]. When unexplained leukocytosis is due to CDI, diarrhea typically develops one to two days later. ( See \"Approach to the patient with neutrophilia\", section on 'Infection' .) Severe </cover>and fulminant colitis — Clinical manifestations of severe colitis include diarrhea, lower quadrant or diffuse abdominal pain, abdominal distention, fever, hypovolemia, lactic acidosis, hypoalbuminemia, elevated creatinine, and marked leukocytosis (white blood cell count up to 40,000 cells/mL or higher) [ 5,8,9 ]. Criteria proposed for severe CDI (based on expert opinion) include white blood cell count >15,000 cells/mL or serum creatinine ≥1.5 mg/dL;\" , \"start_char\": ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1022305782#3_2091726498","rank":1,"relevance":2}]}}
