{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_1298308052#0_2372282759","relevance":2,"reason":"This Mayo Clinic page is dedicated to high white blood cell count and explains that it usually indicates infection, drug reaction, bone marrow disease, or immune disorder, and advises working with a doctor. However, it does not specifically address WBC over 15,000, danger, or lack of treatment.","learned":"Authoritative evidence: a high WBC count is a sign of an underlying condition such as infection, medication effect, bone marrow disease, or immune disorder; the cause should be evaluated with a clinician."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"high white blood cell count\" (+ \"seek medical\" \"see a doctor\" \"work with your doctor\") (+ treatment infection leukemia))","reason":"Look for patient-facing pages that combine high WBC with explicit medical evaluation/treatment advice, which more directly answers whether it is safe to leave untreated."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"high white blood cell count\" (+ \"seek medical\" \"see a doctor\" \"work with your doctor\") (+ treatment infection leukemia))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_59_571972637#1_1397906722","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2931","rank":1,"snippet":"\"Answer There are a few different causes of an elevated white blood cell count. Since the white blood cell are involved in fighting off infections in the body, almost always a white blood cell count that is elevated means that your son has just been fighting off an infection. The most common scenario in which this occurs is that you bring your child to the pediatrician for a regular checkup visit, and the doctor orders routine checkup blood work that comes back with a <cover>high white blood cell count. See a doctor who can help Find Pediatricians near you This typically just means that your son has just gotten over some viral infection (</cover>like a common cold), and the usual thing to be done is to just repeat the blood work in a few weeks to make sure that the count has come down. In rare cases, a high white blood cell count can be a sign of a serious problem, most typically a blood cancer like a leukemia or a lymphoma. Even though this is not at all common, this is the reason that your pediatrician is being cautious and checking additional blood work. It will be important "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"13/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_59_571972637#1_1397906722","relevance":3,"reason":"This doctor-answer page is dedicated to an elevated/high white blood cell count and directly addresses danger and management: it is often due to a recent infection and may only need repeat blood work, but rarely can indicate serious blood cancers, which is why follow-up testing is important.","learned":"A high WBC count is not automatically dangerous or always treated immediately; clinicians often repeat testing, but persistent or unexplained elevation needs evaluation because rare serious causes include leukemia/lymphoma."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2931","reason":"This query is producing direct patient-facing evidence about high WBC count, medical follow-up, and serious causes, so the next results may include more grade-3 answers."}}
