{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2932"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_11_1429319552#3_3022860411","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2932","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_11_1429319552#3_3022860411\" , \"end_char\": 2419.000000 , \"headings\": \"\\\" I had CBC done today and was told I have a WBC over 15000. What does that mean? \\\" Zocdoc Answers Medical questions & health advice by licensed doctors \\\" I had CBC done today and was told I have a <cover>WBC over 15000. What does that mean? \" Question Answer See a doctor </cover>who can help Need more info? \" , \"segment\": \"See a doctor who can help Find Infectious disease specialists near you I recommend that you speak with your doctor. The fact that you have hot flashes, sore joints, fatigue, swollen lymph nodes, and other symptoms all can indicate that some sort of infection is ongoing. Your doctor should be able to help you by recommending the appropriate treatment and testing that is needed to determine what is causing your symptoms. Often, doctors will prescribe an antibiotic before knowing exactly what is causing the infection (ie, whether the infection is from a virus or a bacteria). This can often be considered empiric therapy, which has a "}}
