{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_11_1429319552#1_3022856821","relevance":2,"reason":"This is another chunk of the exact Zocdoc page about WBC over 15000, but the visible passage is mostly the patient question and only begins the answer, so it is relevant but incomplete by itself.","learned":"The Zocdoc exact-threshold document is split into multiple chunks; some chunks contain the direct answer while this one mostly repeats the question."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"white blood cell count\" wbc leukocytosis) (+ \"15,000\" 15000) (+ infection antibiotics treatment) (+ doctor))","reason":"Search for additional exact-threshold evidence connecting WBC around 15,000 with infection and medical treatment/evaluation, while avoiding overfocusing on the same Zocdoc wording."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ \"white blood cell count\" wbc leukocytosis) (+ \"15,000\" 15000) (+ infection antibiotics treatment) (+ doctor))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_46_748555597#2_1575854060","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2933","rank":4,"snippet":"preparation for Surgery 3 Responses You are reading content posted in the Respiratory Disorders Forum \" , \"segment\": \"This could be due to acute stress that can be experienced before surgery. The differential counts the number of cells for each type of WBC such as the segmented neutrophils (SEG), lymphocytes (LYMPH), and monocytes (MOMO). Your differential appears to be normal. This suggests that the WBC count of <cover>15,000 is not a sign of infection. Medicine, not disease, is the most common cause of a high WBC count in this range. Corticosteroids can cause the WBC count to be this high or higher. With corticosteroids the neutrophils tend to be high so it is called neutrophilia. I suggest you discuss this with your doctor </cover>to determine if there is some other cause. Some people just \"normally\" have a WBC count in this range. Often other people in their family will also have a WBC count in this range.\" , \"start_char\": 1217.000000 , \"title\": \"High WBC in preparation for Surgery - Respiratory Disorders - MedHelp\" , \"url\": \"https://www.medhelp.org/posts/","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_11_1429319552#4_3022862229","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_11_1429319552#2_3022858430","rank":2,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_11_1429319552#3_3022860411","rank":3,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"20/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_46_748555597#2_1575854060","relevance":3,"reason":"This forum doctor-style answer directly interprets a WBC count of 15,000, stating that with a normal differential it may not be infection, can be due to stress or corticosteroid medicine, and should be discussed with a doctor to determine the cause. This directly supports that danger/treatment depends on context rather than the number alone.","learned":"A WBC of 15,000 is not automatically dangerous or necessarily infection; medications, stress, or normal variation can cause it, but a doctor should evaluate the cause."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2933","reason":"This exact-threshold query is producing direct evidence about WBC 15,000 interpretation and management, so more results may yield additional grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2933"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_272550136#4_333617661","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2933","rank":5,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_272550136#4_333617661\" , \"end_char\": 4233.000000 , \"headings\": \"How to Reduce White Blood Cells How to Reduce White Blood Cells Method 1 of 3: Diagnosing an Underlying Cause Method 2 of 3: Making Lifestyle Changes Method 3 of 3: Seeking Medical <cover>Treatment Expert Q&A References About This Article Jurdy Dugdale, RN Did this article help you? \" , \"segment\": \"Counts above 100,000 usually mean a more serious condition that your doctor will need to diagnose. This could range from severe bronchitis to leukemia in rare cases. Many pregnant women have a WBC count of up to 15,000 </cover>in the third trimester and after giving birth, which may be normal. {\"smallUrl\":\"https:\\/\\/www.wikihow.com\\/images\\/thumb\\/7\\/73\\/Raise-Blood-Platelet-Level-Naturally-Step-11.jpg\\/v4-460px-Raise-Blood-Platelet-Level-Naturally-Step-11.jpg\",\"bigUrl\":\"\\/images\\/thumb\\/7\\/73\\/Raise-Blood-Platelet-Level-Naturally-Step-11.jpg\\/aid9358249-v4-728px-Raise-Blood-Platelet-Level-Naturally-Step-11.jpg\",\"smallWidth\":460,\"smallHeight\":345,\"bigWidth\":728,\"bigHeight\":546,\"licensing\":\"<div "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"21/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_272550136#4_333617661","relevance":3,"reason":"This article is dedicated to reducing/diagnosing high white blood cells and directly supports interpretation of WBC around 15,000: it says counts above 100,000 are more concerning and need diagnosis, while WBC up to 15,000 can be normal in pregnancy/postpartum. It supports that danger depends on context and underlying cause rather than the number alone.","learned":"A WBC count around 15,000 is not automatically dangerous in every context, but elevated counts should be interpreted with clinical context; much higher counts such as above 100,000 are more concerning and need diagnosis."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2933","reason":"The exact-threshold query continues to yield directly relevant evidence about WBC 15,000, causes, and medical evaluation, so continue to look for more grade-3 results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2933"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_272550136#3_333615496","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2933","rank":6,"snippet":"\"segment\": \"1] X Trustworthy Source American Academy of Family Physicians Organization devoted to improving the health of patients, families, and communities Go to source Counts around 30,000 can be due to physical stress, injuries, allergic reactions, infections, or medication. For example, you may have the flu. Counts between 50,000 and 100,000 usually indicate a serious <cover>infection, such as advanced pneumonia. In a person who has had an organ transplant, it could mean an organ rejection. Additionally, some tumors can cause high WBC counts, which could be either cancerous or benign. Counts above 100,000 usually mean a more serious condition that your doctor will need to diagnose. This could range from severe bronchitis to leukemia in rare cases. Many pregnant women have a WBC count of up to 15,000 </cover>in the third trimester and after giving birth, which may be normal. {\"smallUrl\":\"https:\\/\\/www.wikihow.com\\/images\\/thumb\\/7\\/73\\/Raise-Blood-Platelet-Level-Naturally-Step-11.jpg\\/v4-460px-Raise-Blood-Platelet-Level-Naturally-Step-11.jpg\",\"bigUrl\":\"\\/images\\/thumb\\/7\\/73\\/Raise-Blood-Platelet-Level-Naturally-Step-11.jpg\\/aid9358249-v4-728px-Raise-Blood-Platelet-Level-Naturally-Step-11.jpg\",\""}}
{"request":{"judgments":"22/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_58_272550136#3_333615496","relevance":3,"reason":"This high-WBC article directly discusses how different WBC levels map to seriousness: around 30,000 may occur with stress, injury, allergy, infection, or medication; 50,000–100,000 often suggests serious infection or other major conditions; above 100,000 needs diagnosis; and up to 15,000 can be normal in late pregnancy/postpartum. It directly supports that WBC over 15,000 is not automatically dangerous but depends on context and cause.","learned":"WBC 15,000 is only mildly/moderately elevated in many contexts; danger depends on cause, symptoms, and much higher counts are generally more concerning."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ leukocytosis evaluation management \"15,000\" (+ infection malignancy \"white blood cell\"))","reason":"Search for more authoritative clinical guidance on evaluating leukocytosis with the 15,000 threshold, infection/malignancy causes, and management implications."}}
