{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ leukocytosis \"11,000\" \"30,000\" (+ infection stress medication malignancy))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_1430758418#1_2641920495","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2935","rank":1,"snippet":"blood cells (WBC) or leukocytes play an important role in the body’s immune system to fight against infections. Various types of WBCs include the neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils and basophils. The neutrophils are the most numerous, followed by the lymphocytes. These cells are produced by the bone marrow. A condition with an increased count of more than <cover>11,000 leukocytes per cubic mm of adult human blood is termed as leukocytosis. ( 1 ✔) The WBC count is often increased as a reaction of the bone marrow to infection or inflammation. Certain bacterial and viral infection can trigger leukocytosis. Leukocytosis is also related to strong immune reactions like asthma and other allergy attacks. Some of the most serious and life-threatening cases of leukocytosis are associated with the diseases of the bone marrow. An elevated WBC count of greater than 30,000 </cover>per cubic mm of the adult human blood could indicate an underlying bone marrow disorder including leukemia or blood cancer.\" , \"start_char\": 477.000000 , \"title\": \"Leukocytosis - Causes Symptoms Diagnosis Treatment\" , \"url\": \"http://www.medindia.net/patients/patientinfo/leukocytosis.htm\""}}
{"request":{"judgments":"23/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_07_1430758418#1_2641920495","relevance":3,"reason":"This patient-facing article is dedicated to leukocytosis causes, symptoms, diagnosis, and treatment. It directly explains that adult WBC above 11,000 is leukocytosis, is often a reaction to infection or inflammation, but serious/life-threatening cases can involve bone marrow disease; higher counts such as above 30,000 may indicate leukemia or another marrow disorder. This directly supports that WBC over 15,000 should not be ignored and danger depends on the cause.","learned":"WBC over 15,000 is leukocytosis; common causes include infection/inflammation, but potentially life-threatening bone marrow diseases are possible, especially with higher or unexplained counts."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2935","reason":"This query produced a strong dedicated leukocytosis article with useful seriousness and treatment context; continuing may find more grade-3 evidence about evaluation and danger depending on cause."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2935"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_46_953210318#1_2076279268","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2935","rank":2,"snippet":"blood cells (WBC) or leukocytes play an important role in the body’s immune system to fight against infections. Various types of WBCs include the neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils and basophils. The neutrophils are the most numerous, followed by the lymphocytes. These cells are produced by the bone marrow. A condition with an increased count of more than <cover>11,000 leukocytes per cubic mm of adult human blood is termed as leukocytosis. ( 1 ✔) The WBC count is often increased as a reaction of the bone marrow to infection or inflammation. Certain bacterial and viral infection can trigger leukocytosis. Leukocytosis is also related to strong immune reactions like asthma and other allergy attacks. Some of the most serious and life-threatening cases of leukocytosis are associated with the diseases of the bone marrow. An elevated WBC count of greater than 30,000 </cover>per cubic mm of the adult human blood could indicate an underlying bone marrow disorder including leukemia or blood cancer.\" , \"start_char\": 556.000000 , \"title\": \"Leukocytosis - Causes Symptoms Diagnosis Treatment\" , \"url\": \"https://www.medindia.net/patients/patientinfo/leukocytosis.htm\""}}
