{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_45_1211172326#18_2442354463","relevance":1,"reason":"This result only cites the AAFP article 'Evaluation of Patients with Leukocytosis' in a references list on a general white-blood-cell page; it does not itself answer whether WBC over 15,000 is dangerous or untreated.","learned":"The AAFP leukocytosis evaluation article exists in the corpus, but this result is only a citation rather than usable evidence."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"evaluation of patients with leukocytosis\" \"white blood cell count\" (+ infection inflammation malignancy))","reason":"Search the exact AAFP article title with WBC and cause terms to retrieve the article itself or a content chunk with direct evaluation guidance."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"evaluation of patients with leukocytosis\" \"white blood cell count\" (+ infection inflammation malignancy))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_31_1728966847#1_3517937790","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2940","rank":1,"snippet":"An elevated white blood cell count has many potential etiologies, including malignant and nonmalignant causes. It is important to use age- and pregnancy-specific normal ranges for the white blood cell count. A repeat complete blood count with peripheral smear may provide helpful information, such as types and maturity of white blood cells, uniformity of white blood cells, and toxic granulations. The leukocyte differential may show eosinophilia in parasitic or allergic conditions, or it may reveal lymphocytosis in childhood viral illnesses. Leukocytosis is a common sign of infection, particularly bacterial, and should prompt physicians to identify other signs and symptoms of <cover>infection. The peripheral white blood cell count can double within hours after certain stimuli because of the large bone marrow storage and intravascularly marginated pools of neutrophils. Stressors capable of causing an acute leukocytosis include surgery, exercise, trauma, and emotional stress. Other nonmalignant etiologies of leukocytosis include certain medications, asplenia, smoking, obesity, and chronic inflammatory conditions.\" , \"start_char\": 479.000000 , \"title\": \"Evaluation of Patients with Leukocytosis - </cover>American Family Physician\" , \"url\": \"https://www.aafp.org/afp/2015/1201/p1004.html\" }"}}
