{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_13_1670989796#3_3657118816","relevance":2,"reason":"This clinical guideline excerpt directly uses WBC ≥15,000 as a marker of severe C. difficile infection and provides recommended antibiotic treatment, supporting that this threshold can indicate serious disease in that context. However, it is disease-specific and not a general answer to whether WBC over 15,000 is dangerous without treatment.","learned":"In C. difficile infection, leukocytosis/WBC ≥15,000 is classified as severe disease and guides antibiotic treatment choice."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ leukocytosis (+ untreated \"without treatment\" complications dangerous) (+ infection leukemia serious))","reason":"Search directly for evidence about what can happen if leukocytosis/high WBC is left untreated, including serious infectious or malignant causes, to answer the user's untreated-danger concern."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ leukocytosis (+ untreated \"without treatment\" complications dangerous) (+ infection leukemia serious))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1980712231#1_3524675242","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2942","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1980712231#1_3524675242\" , \"end_char\": 2321.000000 , \"headings\": \"Leukocytosis Leukocytosis Perioperative Evaluation of Patients with Hematologic Disorders LEUKOCYTOSIS Preoperative Considerations Intrapartum Complications B. Laboratory Findings Postpartum Biomedical Concerns: Breastfeeding C. Laboratory Studies Benign white blood cell and platelet disorders Abstract Leukocytosis and Leukopenia Nonhematologic Malignancy Disorders of White Blood Cells<cover>Leukocytosis Inflammatory and Purpuric Eruptions Laboratory Tests and Histopathology Complications of Catheter Ablation of Cardiac Arrhythmias Detection Volume 1 Suspected Infection</cover>Disorders of White Blood Cells Recommended publications Featured Authors \" , \"segment\": \"Moderate leukocytosis, involving mature white cells, carries no significant increase in perioperative risk. In most situations, leukocytosis requires no specific therapy except that of the underlying disorder. If the leukocytosis is related to the effects of leukemia, obviously the underlying illness and its therapy are of paramount importance in determining the perioperative risk. View chapter Purchase book Read full chapter URL: https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B978141602385250007X Intrapartum Complications Kent Petrie MD, ... Lee T. Dresang MD, in Family Medicine Obstetrics (Third Edition), 2008 B. Laboratory Findings Leukocytosis, defined "}}
