{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2942"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1980712231#0_3524672490","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2942","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_51_1980712231#0_3524672490\" , \"end_char\": 1796.000000 , \"headings\": \"Leukocytosis Leukocytosis Perioperative Evaluation of Patients with Hematologic Disorders LEUKOCYTOSIS Preoperative Considerations Intrapartum Complications B. Laboratory Findings Postpartum Biomedical Concerns: Breastfeeding C. Laboratory Studies Benign white blood cell and platelet disorders Abstract Leukocytosis and Leukopenia Nonhematologic Malignancy Disorders of White Blood Cells<cover>Leukocytosis Inflammatory and Purpuric Eruptions Laboratory Tests and Histopathology Complications of Catheter Ablation of Cardiac Arrhythmias Detection Volume 1 Suspected Infection</cover>Disorders of White Blood Cells Recommended publications Featured Authors \" , \"segment\": \"Leukocytosis - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics Leukocytosis Leukocytosis is defined as a white blood cell count greater than 100 × 109/L.7 It has been reported to occur in up to 20% of childhood leukemias. From: Comprehensive Pediatric Hospital Medicine, 2007 Download as PDF About this page Perioperative Evaluation of Patients with Hematologic Disorders FREDERICK M. FELLIN MD, in Medical Management of the Surgical Patient (Third Edition), 2008 LEUKOCYTOSIS Preoperative Considerations Leukocytosis is usually an indicator of an underlying inflammatory, infectious, or neoplastic process. Although lymphocytosis is most often associated with "}}
