{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_714777078#0_1238794381","relevance":1,"reason":"This result is mostly a hematology/oncology navigation page focused on leukopenia, with leukocytosis only listed among other conditions. It does not answer whether WBC over 15,000 is dangerous without treatment.","learned":"Broad leukocytosis causes/symptoms/treatment queries can retrieve clinic navigation pages rather than substantive evidence."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ leukocytosis \"treatment depends\" \"underlying cause\" (+ infection leukemia inflammation))","reason":"Search for direct patient-facing statements that treatment for leukocytosis/high WBC depends on the underlying cause, which directly answers whether an elevated WBC count itself is dangerous or untreated causes may be dangerous."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ leukocytosis \"treatment depends\" \"underlying cause\" (+ infection leukemia inflammation))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_432100443#1_888816875","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q2945","rank":1,"snippet":"it is normal or abnormal. Your body can have an abnormal white blood cell count from a high number of white blood cells (leukocytosis) or a low number of white blood cells (leukopenia). There are many different causes for an abnormal white blood cell count ranging from a common cold to a genetic disorder. Successful <cover>treatment depends on the cause of your abnormal count. What are the symptoms of an abnormal white blood cell count? A high number of white blood cells (leukocytosis) typically does not cause symptoms in and of itself, and any symptoms, such as fever, hives and breathing problems, are typically due to the underlying cause of the abnormal white blood cell count. You may have no symptoms if your high count is related to stress or a reaction to a medication. In rare and extreme cases (typically associated with leukemia), </cover>your blood can thicken (hyperviscosity syndrome) causing stroke, bleeding, vision and breathing problems. A low number of white blood cells (leukopenia) will not produce any symptoms.\" , \"start_char\": 412.000000 , \"title\": \"Abnormal White Blood Cell Count | Parkview Cancer Institute\" , \"url\""}}
{"request":{"judgments":"43/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_432100443#1_888816875","relevance":3,"reason":"This cancer institute page directly answers the core question: an abnormal/high WBC count has many causes, successful treatment depends on the cause, leukocytosis itself typically causes no symptoms, symptoms are usually due to the underlying cause, and rare extreme leukemia-associated cases can cause dangerous hyperviscosity complications. This directly supports that WBC over 15,000 is not automatically dangerous by itself, but untreated underlying causes can be dangerous.","learned":"High WBC/leukocytosis usually is a sign of an underlying condition rather than a dangerous symptom by itself; treatment depends on the cause, and rare extreme leukemia-related leukocytosis can be dangerous."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q2945","reason":"This query produced a strong direct answer about treatment depending on the underlying cause and danger in rare extreme cases, so the next results may provide additional grade-3 patient-facing evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q2945"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_01_1297686175#4_1726503009","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q2945","rank":2,"snippet":"small intestine (bile). Acalculous cholecystitis Symptoms The symptoms of acalculous cholecystitis are largely the same as acute acalculous cholecystitis. Some of the symptoms are given below; Unexplained fever Abdominal distension Tenderness of the abdomen Abdominal discomfort or pain, usually in the right upper quadrant (RUQ) Elevated white blood cell count (<cover>leukocytosis) Vomiting, loss of appetite, weakness and malaise are non-specific symptoms of acute cholecystitis. In acalculous cholecystitis, these symptoms are more likely due to the underlying cause of gallbladder inflammation. Tachycardia Palpable gallbladder or fullness of the RUQ (30-40% of patients) Jaundice (~15% of patients) Acalculous cholecystitis Causes Acalculous cholecystitis causes are as under; Dengue fever Typhoid Sclerosing cholangiopathy Hepatitis B Hepatitis A Juvenile systemic lupus erythematosus Plasmodium falciparum Acute Epstein-Barr viral infection in a person with Gilbert syndrome Granular cell tumor of the common hepatic duct After cardiopulmonary resuscitation Acalculous cholecystitis Treatment Acalculous cholecystitis treatment depends </cover>on the severity of the condition and the presence or absence of complications. Cholecystectomy is most commonly performed using a tiny video camera mounted at the end of a flexible tube.\" , \"start_char\": 2669.000000 , \"title\": \"Acalculous cholecystitis "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"44/50"}}
